/* data/learn-c/phy-12-11.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-11.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-11"] = {"11.1":{"s":"A freely hung bar magnet points north-south. This chapter asks why some materials are magnetic and others are not. It shows how materials respond to a magnetic field. It also covers permanent magnets, electromagnets and magnetic shields.","x":["A bar magnet that is hung freely by a thread comes to rest pointing roughly north and south. This simple fact is the basis of the compass. It also tells us that the Earth itself behaves like a big magnet.","This chapter asks why some materials are magnetic and others are not, and how a material responds to a magnetic field. It starts with the torque on a magnet in a field, then looks at the origin of magnetism in atoms, defines magnetisation and magnetic intensity, and groups materials as diamagnetic, paramagnetic and ferromagnetic. It then studies hysteresis, the choice of materials for permanent magnets and electromagnets, and magnetic shielding.","Study tip: learn the three kinds of material in a table: the sign and the size of the susceptibility, the effect of temperature, and an example for each."],"e":"A compass needle is a small magnet pivoted at its centre. The Earth's field turns it until it lines up with the field, so its north end points towards the Earth's geographic north."},"11.2":{"s":"Put a magnetic dipole of moment m in a uniform field B. It feels a torque τ = mB sin θ that tries to line it up with the field. Its potential energy is U = −mB cos θ, lowest when aligned. If disturbed slightly, a freely pivoted magnet oscillates about the field direction.","f":[{"x":"tau = m*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a magnet of moment m in a uniform field B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.251"},{"x":"U_m = -m*B*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy: least (-mB) when aligned with B, most (+mB) when opposite.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.252"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/(m*B))","n":"Period of small angular oscillations; I = moment of inertia of the magnet.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.253"}],"w":["A stronger magnet or a stronger field gives FASTER oscillations (shorter period)."],"x":["A magnetic dipole, such as a small bar magnet or a current loop, has a magnetic moment m. In a uniform magnetic field B, the net force on it is zero, but there is a torque, τ = m × B, with the magnitude τ = mB sin θ, where θ is the angle between m and B. The torque turns the dipole until it is along the field. It is zero at θ = 0° and 180°, and the largest at 90°.","The potential energy of the dipole in the field is U = −mB cos θ. It is the lowest, −mB, when the dipole is along the field (stable equilibrium), and the highest, +mB, when it is opposite to the field (unstable equilibrium). The work done in turning the dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ is mB(cos θ₁ − cos θ₂).","If a freely pivoted magnet is turned by a small angle and released, it oscillates about the field direction. For small angles, the torque is −mBθ, so the motion is simple harmonic, with the time period T = 2π√(I/(mB)), where I is the moment of inertia about the pivot. A stronger magnet or a stronger field gives a shorter period, and so, faster oscillations.","Common mistake: using cos in place of sin for the torque, or forgetting the minus sign in U. The unit of m is A m²."],"e":"A magnet of 2.0 A m², with I = 1.5 × 10⁻⁶ kg m², oscillates in a field of 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ T. T = 2π√(1.5 × 10⁻⁶/(6 × 10⁻⁵)) = 2π × 0.158 = 0.99 s."},"11.3":{"s":"Magnetism in materials comes from electrons. An electron orbiting the nucleus is a tiny current loop with a magnetic moment, and the electron's spin adds another. In atoms where all electrons are paired, these moments cancel. Atoms with unpaired electrons have a net magnetic moment.","f":[{"x":"m_orb = (e/(2*m_e))*L","n":"Orbital magnetic moment from the orbital angular momentum L; e/(2 m_e) = gyromagnetic ratio.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.3, p.254"},{"x":"mu_B = e*h/(4*pi*m_e)","n":"Bohr magneton = 9.274 × 10^-24 A m^2, the moment of the first Bohr orbit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.3, p.254"}],"w":["The electron is negative, so its orbital magnetic moment points opposite to its angular momentum."],"x":["The magnetism of materials comes from the electrons in their atoms. An electron going round a nucleus is a tiny loop of current, and it has an orbital magnetic moment m = (e/2mₑ)L, where L is the orbital angular momentum. As the electron is negative, this moment points opposite to L. The electron also has a spin, and with it a spin magnetic moment.","The smallest unit of the atomic magnetic moment is the Bohr magneton, μ_B = eh/(4πmₑ) = 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m². It is the orbital moment of an electron in the first Bohr orbit.","In most atoms, the electrons are in pairs with opposite spins and opposite orbital motion, and their magnetic moments cancel. Such atoms have no net moment, and the material is diamagnetic. Atoms with unpaired electrons have a net magnetic moment, and the material can be paramagnetic or ferromagnetic. The magnetic moments of the nuclei are about 2000 times smaller than those of electrons, and are mostly ignored.","Common mistake: thinking that the electron's moment is in the same direction as its angular momentum. The charge is negative, so the two are opposite."],"e":"Hydrogen in the ground state: the orbital moment is zero for the 1s electron, and the spin moment is about one Bohr magneton, 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m². An atom with all its electrons paired, such as helium, has no net moment."},"11.4":{"s":"Magnetization (M) is the net magnetic moment per unit volume of a material. Magnetic intensity (H) describes the applied field. They are linked by B = μ₀(H + M). Magnetic susceptibility χ = M/H tells how easily a material is magnetized, and the relative permeability is 1 + χ.","f":[{"x":"M = m_net/V","n":"Magnetization: net magnetic moment per unit volume (A/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.255"},{"x":"B = mu_0*(H + M)","n":"H = magnetic intensity (A/m), set by the free current, e.g. H = n I in a solenoid.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"},{"x":"M = chi*H","n":"chi = magnetic susceptibility (no unit).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"},{"x":"mu_r = 1 + chi","n":"Relative permeability; mu = mu_0 mu_r and B = mu H.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"}],"w":["H and M have the same unit (A/m); B is in tesla."],"x":["When a material is put in a magnetic field, its atomic magnetic moments are lined up to some extent. The magnetisation M is the net magnetic moment per unit volume: M = m_net/V, in A/m. The magnetic intensity H describes the field due to the external currents alone, and its unit is also A/m. The total magnetic field B in the material is B = μ₀(H + M), in tesla.","For many materials, M is proportional to H: M = χH, where χ is the magnetic susceptibility, a number without a unit. It tells how easily the material is magnetised. Then B = μ₀(1 + χ)H = μH. The factor μᵣ = 1 + χ is the relative permeability, and μ = μ₀μᵣ is the permeability of the material.","A material with a large χ, like iron, strengthens the field a great deal. For a long solenoid with n turns per metre and a current I, H = nI. With a core of relative permeability μᵣ, B = μ₀μᵣnI.","Common mistake: mixing the units of B, H and M. H and M are in A/m, and B is in tesla. Also, B = μ₀H is only true in vacuum, or in air."],"e":"A solenoid with 1000 turns/m and 0.50 A has H = 500 A/m. With a soft iron core of μᵣ = 500, B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 500 × 500 = 0.314 T. In air, it would be only 6.3 × 10⁻⁴ T."},"11.5":{"s":"Diamagnetic materials (copper, bismuth, water) are weakly repelled; χ is small and negative. Paramagnetic materials (aluminium, oxygen) are weakly attracted; χ is small and positive and falls as temperature rises (Curie's law). Ferromagnetic materials (iron, nickel, cobalt) are strongly attracted because of domains. Above the Curie temperature they become paramagnetic.","f":[{"x":"chi = C*mu_0/T","n":"Curie's law for a paramagnet: susceptibility falls as temperature rises.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.5, p.259"},{"x":"chi = C/(T - T_C)","n":"Ferromagnet above its Curie temperature T_C, where it behaves as a paramagnet.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.5, p.261"}],"w":["Diamagnetic χ is small and negative; paramagnetic small and positive; ferromagnetic very large and positive.","A diamagnetic rod sets at right angles to the field; a paramagnetic rod along it."],"x":["Materials are put in three groups by the way that they respond to a magnetic field. Diamagnetic materials, such as copper, bismuth, water, gold and silver, are weakly repelled by a magnet. Their susceptibility is small and negative (about −10⁻⁵) and does not depend on the temperature. A diamagnetic rod, in a field, sets at right angles to the field, and moves towards the weaker part of the field. All atoms show some diamagnetism, but it is seen only when the atoms have no net moment.","Paramagnetic materials, such as aluminium, sodium, oxygen and platinum, are weakly attracted. Their atoms have permanent moments, which the field lines up. The susceptibility is small and positive, and it falls when the temperature rises, in agreement with Curie's law, χ = C/T. A paramagnetic rod sets along the field.","Ferromagnetic materials, such as iron, nickel, cobalt and some alloys, are strongly attracted. Their susceptibility is very large and positive, because they have domains, which are small regions where the atomic moments are all lined up. In a field, the domains grow and turn along it. Above a certain temperature, the Curie temperature, the domains break up, and the material becomes paramagnetic, with χ = C/(T − T_C).","Common mistake: thinking that a diamagnetic substance is not affected by a field. It is, but weakly, and by repulsion."],"e":"Iron (T_C = 1043 K) is ferromagnetic at room temperature. At 1200 K, it is paramagnetic. A paramagnetic salt with χ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ at 300 K has χ = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ at 400 K."},"11.6":{"s":"When a ferromagnet is magnetized and demagnetized, B lags behind H. The B–H loop is called hysteresis. Retentivity is the magnetism left when H is zero. Coercivity is the reverse H needed to remove it. The area of the loop is the energy lost as heat per cycle.","w":["Retentivity is the B left when H = 0; coercivity is the reverse H needed to bring B to 0."],"x":["When a ferromagnetic material is taken through a cycle of magnetising and demagnetising, the magnetic field B in it lags behind the intensity H. The graph of B against H is a loop, called the hysteresis loop (the word means lagging behind). It is a result of the domains, which do not return to their original positions when the field is removed.","Two important values: the retentivity (or remanence) is the value of B that is left in the material when H is reduced to zero. The coercivity is the reverse value of H that is needed to bring B back to zero. A third value is the saturation, the highest magnetisation.","The area of the hysteresis loop is the energy that is lost as heat, in each cycle, in a unit volume of the material. So when the field is changing all the time, as in the core of a transformer or a motor, a material with a thin loop, that is, a small area, should be used, so that the loss is small. A material with a wide loop is used where a lasting magnet is needed.","Common mistake: mixing up retentivity and coercivity. The retentivity is a value of B, at H = 0. The coercivity is a value of H, at B = 0."],"e":"A soft iron core in a transformer goes round its loop 50 times every second. If the area of its loop is small, then the heat lost per second is small. A steel core would have a wide loop, and it would get very hot."},"11.7":{"s":"Soft iron has high permeability and low coercivity, so it magnetizes and demagnetizes easily: good for electromagnets and transformer cores. Hard materials such as steel and alnico keep their magnetism (high retentivity and coercivity): good for permanent magnets.","w":["Electromagnet core: soft iron (thin loop, low loss). Permanent magnet: hard material (wide loop)."],"x":["Soft iron has a high permeability and a low coercivity. It is magnetised easily by a small field, and loses its magnetism easily when the field is removed. It has a thin hysteresis loop, so the energy loss is small. So it is used for the cores of electromagnets, transformers, motors and generators.","An electromagnet is a coil with a soft iron core. The core increases the field by a factor of the order of hundreds or thousands, compared with the coil without the core. The magnetism can be switched on and off, and its strength can be changed by changing the current. Electromagnets are used in cranes for lifting scrap iron, in electric bells, in relays and in loudspeakers.","A permanent magnet should keep its magnetism for a long time, and should not be demagnetised by stray fields, by heat or by a shock. So it needs a high retentivity and a high coercivity, with a wide loop. Hard magnetic materials such as steel, alnico (an alloy of aluminium, nickel and cobalt) and ferrites are used.","Common mistake: using the same material for both purposes. For an electromagnet, a thin loop (soft iron). For a permanent magnet, a wide loop (steel or alnico)."],"e":"The core of a transformer is made of thin plates of soft iron with silicon. The magnets in a loudspeaker and in a compass needle are made of alnico or steel, which keep their magnetism."},"11.8":{"s":"Put a closed shell of soft iron in a magnetic field. The field lines crowd into its walls, so very little field reaches the space inside. This magnetic shielding protects sensitive instruments, such as watches and meters, from outside fields.","x":["A magnetic field cannot be stopped by an ordinary wall, since all the materials have a permeability that is close to that of air. But a material with a very high permeability can guide the field lines through itself. This gives us a way of shielding a space from a magnetic field.","Place a closed hollow shell of soft iron in an external magnetic field. The field lines prefer to go through the iron, which has a very high permeability, and they crowd into the walls. Very few lines pass through the hollow space inside, so the field there is very small. This is called magnetic shielding.","The thicker the wall, and the higher the permeability, the better the shielding. It is used to protect sensitive instruments, such as watches, moving-coil meters, cathode ray tubes and some electronic parts, from stray magnetic fields. Some metal cases of watches, and the covers of cables, work in this way.","Note the difference from electric shielding. A hollow conductor protects the inside from an electric field completely. A hollow shell of iron reduces the magnetic field a great deal, but not to exactly zero."],"e":"A sensitive meter is placed inside a thick box of soft iron. A strong magnet outside the box makes almost no change in the reading of the meter, since the field lines go along the walls of the box."}};
